;; spec:   §6 (syntax §3.16)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 5
;; audit:  clean
;; The macro's body builds a cycle through a cell at expansion time and
;; returns its argument. The spec permits that leak:
;;   ownership.md §6: "an object unreachable except through a cycle of
;;   cells is garbage. The implementation is not required to reclaim it
;;   ... A leaked cycle is not a memory-safety violation, and the
;;   reference interpreter reports such leaks separately from every other
;;   audit failure (case 15)."
;;   syntax §3.16: the macro body "is ordinary fibber ... evaluated by the
;;   reference interpreter's evaluator ... macro-time evaluation is then
;;   the same executable spec as run time".
;; At run time case 15 (the same kind of leak) is accepted with
;; audit: leak-cycle, so the expansion must succeed and main returns 5
;; with a clean run-time audit.
;;
;; Observed (fibref run):
;;   rejected:
;;   003-macro-run-leaking-a-cycle-rejected.fib:33:23: macro knot failed:
;;   the audit of its run is not clean: clean=false leak-cycles=4 leaks=0 errors=0
;; Diagnosis: eval (crates/fibref/src/eval/macros.rs, run_macro) treats
;; any unclean macro-time audit as an expansion error, including the
;; leak-cycle outcome the spec allows; a compiler that JITs the macro
;; module (syntax §3.16) would expand this program. Only leaks and
;; errors, not cycle leaks, should fail an expansion (or the spec should
;; say that macro runs must not leak cycles).
(defmacro knot (x)
  (let ((c (cell [])))
    (do (set! c (conj @c (fn () (count @c)))) x)))

(defun main () -> i64 (knot 5))
